<h2>Dance and the Body</h2>
<p><em>Two very different ideas of what the body can express</em></p>
<p>This unit traces the evolution from classical ballet to modern dance. In the Watch &amp; Listen section of the lesson you can see two very different approaches to dance: Martha Graham's <em>Lamentation</em> (1930), a modern dance solo performed seated inside a tube of fabric, and a classical ballet performance of <em>Swan Lake</em>. Watch both clips, then compare the two approaches.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>The prompt</strong></p>
<p>How does each use the body differently? How does each define beauty in movement? This unit explains that modern dance returned to "natural" movement as a reaction against ballet's formalized vocabulary. Based on what you see in these two clips, what do you think each tradition values about the human body and its capacity for expression?</p>
</div></div>
<p>Support your comparison with specific moments: the shapes of the body, the use of the floor or lifts, the relationship to music, and the quality of the energy. If you were choreographing a new dance about a feeling of your own, which tradition would you start from, and why?</p>
